{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>He set the foolish.</b> The commentators said it should have been with shin [i.e., לשבת], the meaning being that men of understanding deserve to sit in high places, and the rich the opposite. The meaning itself is correct, but it is not the sense of the verse, which is what comes before it and after it. Rather, its meaning is: He set the foolish man — that is, the fool — to be in high places. It is possible that סכל is an adjective noun, like ילך and הלך, and the meaning is many, like גדולים and עשירים, who have rank in the world, as he said in the book of Proverbs: the rich over the poor rule (Proverbs 22:7), those who sit in lowliness."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:53.085784881Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}